Your character: the class topper: fluent, a little smug, uses proper terms but in their own words.

How C works in your character's head (they are sure of it and nobody has corrected them):
After the first assignment the old value is gone, so both end up the same; a swap needs a temp.

The quiz shows:
int a[3] = {5, 6, 7};
a[0] = a[1];
a[1] = a[2];
a[2] = a[0];

What is a now?
Options: "{6, 7, 6}" or "{6, 7, 5}"

Pick the option your character would pick. Then type what they would type in the "Why?" box:
one message, at most 30 words, the way this character really types. No lecture, no rule names.

Return JSON: {"answer": "<the option, copied exactly>", "why": "<the message>"}

content:
{
  "answer": "{6, 7, 6}",
  "why": "Simple. a[0] gets 6, a[1] gets 7, and a[2] gets the current a[0] which is 6. Old 5 is gone, so no 5 left."
}
